      <h1>Bigme B6 Pro Review: 75 FPS 6-Inch Color E-Reader With 4G</h1>
      <p class="meta"><time datetime="2026-08-24">Published August 24, 2026</time></p>

      <div set:html={`
<div>
<h2 id="news-hook">Bigme Just Launched a Pocket-Sized Color E-Reader That Refreshes at 75 FPS</h2>

<p>On August 18, 2026, Android e-reader maker <a href="https://www.bigme.com/" rel="noopener">Bigme</a> launched the <strong>B6 Pro</strong>, a 6-inch premium color e-reader that pushes E Ink color refresh rates further than almost anything on the shelf. The headline feature, repeated across launch coverage and <a href="https://store.bigme.vip/products/bigme-b6-pro-6premium-color-ebook-reader-with-up-to-75fps" rel="noopener">Bigme's official store listing</a>, is support for refresh speeds up to <strong>75 frames per second (FPS)</strong> &mdash; a step-change over the older 30 FPS you get on most color readers. In the same announcement, Bigme indicated it is boosting frame rates across its wider E Ink lineup, not just this one model.</p>

<p>For EPUB readers, the B6 Pro matters because it belongs to a small but growing category: a genuinely portable, color, Android-powered e-reader that runs reading apps directly and supports far more than a one-ecosystem store. Given Amazon's recent <a href="/blog/amazon-kindle-price-hike-2026/">Kindle price hikes</a> and tightening DRM rules, an open Android color reader at a $269 launch price is worth a close look. Here is a full breakdown of the specs, what the device does well, and where its weaknesses lie.</p>

<h2 id="specs">Bigme B6 Pro: The Full Spec Sheet</h2>

<p>Bigme positions the B6 Pro as a "premium" version of its 6-inch reader, and the spec sheet largely backs that up. It packs a lot of hardware into a device small enough to slip into a pocket or bag, and it runs full Android 14 with the Google Play Store, which means it can install essentially any reading app.</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Spec</th><th>Bigme B6 Pro</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Display</td><td>6-inch Kaleido 3 color E Ink (B/W 300 PPI, color 150 PPI)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Refresh rate</td><td>Up to 75 FPS</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Processor</td><td>MediaTek Dimensity 1080</td></tr>
    <tr><td>RAM / Storage</td><td>8 GB RAM / 256 GB</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Connectivity</td><td>Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, 4G LTE (cellular)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Operating system</td><td>Android 14 with Google Play</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Front light</td><td>Dual color-temperature adjustable</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Handwriting</td><td>Stylus supported (sold separately)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Battery</td><td>2,120 mAh</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Formats</td><td>20+ (EPUB, PDF, MOBI, AZW3, CBZ, TXT and more)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Launch price</td><td>US$269 (US$329 list)</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>A few of these numbers deserve a closer look. The <a href="https://store.bigme.vip/products/bigme-b6-pro-6premium-color-ebook-reader-with-up-to-75fps" rel="noopener">Kaleido 3 screen</a> is the current standard for color E Ink: it renders color at a coarser 150 PPI while keeping monochrome text crisp at 300 PPI. That 300 PPI black-and-white layer is what makes EPUB text look sharp for long reading sessions, while the color layer handles covers, diagrams, comics, and book-highlight colors. The 75 FPS rating applies to the refresh mode that smooths page turns, scrolling, and handwriting on screen, rather than static text pages &mdash; but for anyone who found older color readers sluggish, the difference is immediately noticeable.</p>

<p>The inclusion of <strong>4G LTE</strong> alongside Wi-Fi is the other standout. Few color e-readers ship a cellular radio, and it means the B6 Pro can sync your EPUB library, download books, and fetch news without hunting for Wi-Fi &mdash; genuinely useful on a commute or when traveling. Bigme's official store shows the base price at US$269 with the list price set at US$329, and it offers stylus and case bundle variants at a small premium.</p>

<h2 id="why-color-reader">Why a Color E-Reader, and Why Android Matters for EPUB</h2>

<p>The color-e-reader category has been building momentum all year, and the B6 Pro enters at one of the most interesting moments yet. Our <a href="/blog/color-e-ink-readers-2026/">color E Ink reader guide</a> explains the tradeoffs in detail, but the short version is that color screens make covers, comics, children's picture books, and illustrated docs much more pleasant to read while still offering a paper-like display that is easier on the eyes than a tablet.</p>

<p>What makes Android-based readers like the B6 Pro especially attractive for EPUB lovers is app freedom. Because it runs Android 14 with the Google Play Store and 8 GB of RAM, you are not locked into a single bookstore. You can install the Kindle app, Kobo, PocketBook, Libby and other library apps, Aldiko, Moon+ Reader, or any EPUB reader that suits you &mdash; and switch between them freely. For people who build an EPUB library in <a href="/blog/calibre-library-management-guide-2026/">Calibre</a>, an Android reader sidesteps the sideloading quirks that come with Amazon's walled garden.</p>

<h2 id="epub-reading">How the B6 Pro Reads EPUB Files</h2>

<p>Reading EPUB on the B6 Pro is straightforward because it is a full app-based device rather than a format-restricted e-reader. You have several paths depending on how you manage your library:</p>

<ol>
  <li><strong>Sideload directly.</strong> Connect the B6 Pro to a computer over USB-C (or over cloud file sync), drop EPUB files into a folder, and open them in your chosen reader app. Because it is Android, file management is flexible and most EPUB apps handle full reflow, adjustable fonts, and bookmarks.</li>
  <li><strong>Use an EPUB reader app.</strong> Install a dedicated EPUB reader from Google Play for fine-grained control over typography, dark mode, and dual-page layouts.</li>
  <li><strong>Sync from your Calibre library.</strong> Point Calibre's content server or Send-to-Device at the reader to keep your whole collection in sync, exactly as you would with other open e-readers.</li>
  <li><strong>Download from stores or library apps.</strong> Use Libby for library ebooks, the Kindle or Kobo apps for store purchases, or any other compatible app.</li>
</ol>

<p>Because the device supports 20+ formats out of the box, EPUB files open natively without needing a conversion step. For anyone coming from a Kindle, our <a href="/blog/migrate-kindle-library-to-epub/">guide to migrating a Kindle library to EPUB</a> and our <a href="/blog/epub-to-kindle-formatting-2026/">EPUB formatting guide</a> cover the preparation side, and our <a href="/blog/calibre-epub-to-kindle-workflow-2026/">Calibre-to-Kindle workflow</a> shows the reverse path if you ever need it.</p>

<h2 id="battery-and-caveats">The Tradeoffs: Battery, Weight, and Price</h2>

<p>No device is perfect, and the B6 Pro has a few caveats worth flagging honestly. The most visible concern is the <strong>2,120 mAh battery</strong>. Driving a Dimensity 1080 processor, a 4G baseband, a color screen, and the front light from a fairly small cell is demanding. Bigme's own marketing does not emphasize battery life heavily, and reviewers note it is likely to be the device's weakest area &mdash; especially in color mode with the light on and cellular active. A large EPUB library with frequent page turns will drain it faster than a monochrome reader like the Boox Palma or Kindle would, so heavy users should expect to charge more often.</p>

<p>The second caveat is color resolution. At 150 PPI, color content looks good but not razor-sharp; it is best for covers, comics, and illustration rather than fine color photographs. If color fidelity is your top priority, a larger $300-plus color tablet-style reader with a faster panel may serve better, at the cost of size and heft. The B6 Pro is a pocket-first device, so it trades a bit of color sharpness for portability.</p>

<p>Finally, price and longevity. At a US$269 launch price (US$329 list), the B6 Pro sits above an entry Kindle and below most large color tablets. It is a real investment, so it makes sense to confirm the device fits how you read before committing &mdash; the <a href="/blog/best-ereader-2026-expert-roundup/">2026 e-reader roundup</a> compares it against the field, and our <a href="/blog/kobo-vs-kindle-vs-pocketbook-2026/">Kindle vs. Kobo vs. PocketBook comparison</a> covers the main open alternatives.</p>

<h2 id="how-it-compares">How the B6 Pro Fits the 2026 Color E-Reader Field</h2>

<p>To decide whether the B6 Pro is the right purchase, it helps to see where it sits among the color e-readers of 2026. Bigme is not alone in chasing smooth color scrolling &mdash; the whole category has been pushing toward higher refresh rates and pocket formats this year. The key comparison is against the small color readers from Boox and Meebook, which occupy a similar price-and-size band, and against the Android e-ink phones from Hisense that blur the line between phone and reader.</p>

<p>Our <a href="/blog/meebook-e6-android-ereader-2026/">Meebook E6 review</a> covers another compact Android color e-reader, and our <a href="/blog/hisense-a10-e-ink-phone-epub-2026/">Hisense A10 review</a> looks at the e-ink phone approach; the <a href="/blog/boox-palma-epub-reading-2026/">Boox Palma</a> remains the reference class-leader for pocket monochrome reading. Where the B6 Pro differs is in two ways: it pairs a color panel with a cellular radio that those pocket devices mostly lack, and its 75 FPS refresh is higher than the standard 30 FPS on most of the field. If what you value is pushing EPUB reading along in a pocketable device without depending on Wi-Fi, the B6 Pro is the more communication-ready choice; if battery life is your top concern, a dedicated monochrome reader like the Palma will outlast it easily.</p>

<h2 id="verdict">The Bottom Line</h2>

<p>The Bigme B6 Pro is one of the most feature-dense pocket color e-readers of 2026: a 6-inch Kaleido 3 display with up to 75 FPS refresh, a MediaTek Dimensity 1080, 8 GB of RAM, 256 GB of storage, 4G LTE, and full Android 14 with Google Play &mdash; all for a US$269 launch price. For EPUB readers who want color plus app freedom in a device small enough to travel, it is a compelling open alternative to the Amazon ecosystem, especially now that <a href="/blog/amazon-kindle-price-hike-2026/">Kindle prices have risen</a>. Be realistic about battery life and color resolution, and it earns a solid recommendation as a flexible, format-friendly reading companion.</p>

